From 96d484872c2c438a4d02f47e6390507f587a095c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mike Dodds <mike@oath.tech>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Support unlocked BTS with a 64-bit register offset and a
 memory destination

One form only: `bts r64, m64` (REX.W 0F AB /r; `bts %r15,(%rax)` is
4c 0f ab 38), the single BTS/BTR/BTC in the pinned WireGuard text.

Semantics. Defined when the offset register is below 64 and the eight bytes
at the effective address are mapped data memory; otherwise the step is an
explicit `unimplemented` (never an MMIO load or store). CF receives the old
bit. The quadword is written back with the bit set, unconditionally, as on
hardware. OF, SF, ZF, AF and PF are undefined, by the nondeterministic
treatment register BT already uses. One sequential step; no LOCK prefix
exists to model.

Vendor texts (recorded as read for the consumer project on 2026-11-10; the
Intel page by its statement reviewer, the AMD pages by its coordinator):
- Intel SDM vol. 2A, BTS: CF is the old bit; ZF is unaffected; OF, SF, AF
  and PF are undefined.
- AMD APM vol. 3 (publication 24594, rev. 3.37, pages 132-133), BTS: CF is
  modified; OF, SF, ZF, AF and PF are undefined. For a memory bit base with
  a register bit index, the index is a signed offset over the range of the
  operand size, counted from bit 0 of the addressed byte.

Why this is sound for the consumer's image. Undefined ZF contains
"unaffected", so it is the superset of the two vendors and the right choice
under a universally quantified claim; nothing after the instruction there
reads a flag other than CF. The below-64 restriction declines the offsets at
which hardware addresses another quadword (ea + 8 * (offset asr 6)); in the
image the offset register is masked by `and $0x3f,%r15d` with only a `lea`
between, so the declined case is never reached. A declined step is a
failing step: it cannot make a reaching claim true, and a consumer whose
premise is an actual history must show the step is not declined.

Not accepted, failing closed: register destinations, immediate offsets, the
16- and 32-bit forms (rejected by the parser and, if constructed directly,
`unimplemented`), any LOCK prefix, BTR and BTC.

Tests. `Kraken.X64.BtsTests`: parser acceptance and rejections, both
printers and the AT&T round trip; CF, the written quadword, its two
neighbours, every register and the system flags at all 64 offsets on words
with the bit clear and set; exactly one 8-byte read and one 8-byte write,
also when the bit was already set; each undefined flag taking both values
from either incoming value; offsets 64 and above declined before any
access; unmapped and partly mapped quadwords declined. `test_bts.S` is the
native comparison of CF and the written quadword against the host CPU in
the assembly runner; these are tests, not a proof of ISA fidelity.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
